  summary: 'Chapter 21 examines becoming (saṃbhava) and destruction (vibhava) — the fundamental processes of arising and ceasing. Nāgārjuna shows that inherent becoming and inherent destruction are mutually dependent yet mutually exclusive: each requires the other (destruction requires prior becoming; becoming implies eventual destruction) yet they cannot coexist simultaneously in the same entity. Through systematic analysis — temporal (vv.1-6), categorical (vv.7-9), identity/difference (vv.10-12), tetralemma (vv.13-16), and conclusive (vv.17-21) — he demonstrates that neither becoming nor destruction can be established as inherently real. The resolution: conventional becoming and destruction function through dependent arising, precisely because they are empty of inherent existence.',
